1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
They're made of rope, wood, stone, iron, steel, and the human impulse to reach out and explore.

2
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:24,280
Now, Bridges on Modern Marvels.

3
00:00:47,571 --> 00:00:50,520
We walk a world surrounded by everyday wonders.

4
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:53,040
None greater than our bridges.

5
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:57,740
There is something impressive about a bridge, any bridge.

6
00:00:58,240 --> 00:00:59,960
A problem gracefully solved.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,480
A barrier overcome.

8
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,460
A passage achieved.

9
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,700
Bridges are such an elegant resolution of the age-old human struggle with nature.

10
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:13,800
A bridge does not conquer, exploit, or destroy.

11
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:17,760
It simply, literally, overcomes.

12
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:24,880
Maybe that's why people seem to have an instinctual fondness for bridges.

13
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:27,900
They represent a good side of human nature.

14
00:01:27,901 --> 00:01:38,760
a desire to reach across to unify to connect riches bring together what is separate not just

15
00:01:38,761 --> 00:01:44,820
in the physical plane but in what you could even call a spiritual way to make it possible for us

16
00:01:44,821 --> 00:01:50,620
to do what i think is at the core of of of what it means to be a human which is which is to explore

17
00:01:50,621 --> 00:01:55,820
and to explore one's world and to expand to reach out i think this very simple thing which it is

18
00:01:55,821 --> 00:02:00,600
linking two points, you see, and permitting people go across, has such an unbelievable

19
00:02:00,601 --> 00:02:03,820
power, and it has such an unbelievable force.

20
00:02:03,821 --> 00:02:07,040
I mean, it has even almost a religious sense,

21
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:11,800
you see, and this force, this is something very elementar that everybody can understand.

22
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:18,560
The sheer beauty of bridges, their perfect proportions and elegant lines, is also part

23
00:02:18,561 --> 00:02:19,764
of their appeal.

24
00:02:19,765 --> 00:02:25,180
People love to look at the bridges, and there's something about the math

25
00:02:25,181 --> 00:02:27,600
that is suddenly physically portrayed.

26
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:29,960
It's, I guess, mathematical poetry.

27
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:34,880
And the math is portrayed in that graceful cantilever

28
00:02:34,881 --> 00:02:40,120
and the amazing arch that will only work in a pure mathematical way.

29
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:44,480
Anything else, anything that looks improper will make it fall down.

30
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:49,480
Bridges do on occasion fall down, even pretty ones.

31
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:52,180
The Tacoma Narrows Bridge, for example,

32
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:54,660
which came apart at the seams in 1940,

33
00:02:54,661 --> 00:02:57,400
in a 42-mile-an-hour windstorm

34
00:02:57,401 --> 00:03:01,100
is perhaps the most spectacular 20th century bridge disaster.

35
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:09,620
What happened was a complex phenomenon called flutter

36
00:03:09,621 --> 00:03:15,880
in which different kinds of motion begin to complement each other

37
00:03:15,881 --> 00:03:17,840
and each one excites the other

38
00:03:17,841 --> 00:03:25,360
so that every move gets to be just a little bit larger than the previous one.

39
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:29,860
And there is no turning back once this process starts.

40
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:34,580
In structures, Murphy's Law is a rigorous law.

41
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:37,680
If it can fall down, it will fall down.

42
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:39,060
This bridge could.

43
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:44,700
How do bridge designers go about planning these enormous, complex structures

44
00:03:44,701 --> 00:03:46,880
in such a way as to avoid a collapse?

45
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,800
What are some of the key questions they must ask in developing a design?

46
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:55,600
How much does the bridge itself weigh? It has to hold itself up.

47
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:59,940
It's one of the first things that a bridge engineer will calculate.

48
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:01,240
Can it hold itself up?

49
00:04:01,460 --> 00:04:04,160
And then there's the live load of everything that you put on the bridge.

50
00:04:04,780 --> 00:04:06,480
And you have to think worst case.

51
00:04:06,481 --> 00:04:11,480
What if there's a terrible traffic jam and there was a truck demonstration

52
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:14,160
and every lane is filled with trucks?

53
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:18,360
Then you say, what if I also added all the environmental loads?

54
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:22,100
What if two feet of snow suddenly fell on the bridge at that time?

55
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:23,700
So that's the challenge.

56
00:04:23,701 --> 00:04:31,240
It's the challenge of gravity, of defying gravity, of holding these people, these vehicles, the snow and everything up in the air.

57
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:41,960
The Romans were the first great bridge builders, erecting so many bridges, it was said, that one could traverse the Roman Empire without fording a single stream.

58
00:04:41,961 --> 00:04:48,960
All Roman bridges are arch bridges, the dominant bridge type in the world until the 19th century.

59
00:04:48,961 --> 00:04:53,960
The arch is a wonderfully stable form that works through compression.

60
00:04:53,961 --> 00:04:58,960
That is, any pressure or weight on top of the arch is driven straight down toward the ground,

61
00:04:58,961 --> 00:05:03,960
where it is resisted, reinforcing the stones that make up the arch itself.

62
00:05:03,961 --> 00:05:07,960
The way that an arch is built up is a series of stones.

63
00:05:07,961 --> 00:05:13,860
stones the french had a wonderful word for it they were called vous soires stones that that had

64
00:05:13,861 --> 00:05:20,660
angles to them and the way that you would build an arch you would build a wooden what we call

65
00:05:20,661 --> 00:05:28,260
false work that would resemble your finished arch and then you would begin laying the stones up

66
00:05:28,261 --> 00:05:35,040
till you got to the top that top stone is called the keystone once the keystone's in place you can

67
00:05:35,041 --> 00:05:42,480
remove your scaffolding in your form and your structure will stay in place without falling down.

68
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:49,480
Rome's most amazing bridges were its aqueducts, like the Pond du Gard in Nîmes, France, composed

69
00:05:49,481 --> 00:05:56,880
of three tiers towering 155 feet above the river Gard and helping transport water no less than 31

70
00:05:56,881 --> 00:05:57,581
miles.

71
00:05:57,582 --> 00:06:04,800
Incredibly, the Voussoirs on the bottom and middle tiers of this great structure were cut so

72
00:06:04,801 --> 00:06:08,133
perfectly, but they were laid in place without mortar.

73
00:06:08,134 --> 00:06:10,540
Compression alone has held this perfect

74
00:06:10,541 --> 00:06:13,620
aqueduct together for 2,000 years.

75
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:19,460
What I think is wonderful in the Roman bridges, it is this unbelievable sense of gravity.

76
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:26,080
You see, they are all works, you know, who stays in the landscape with the same naturality

77
00:06:26,081 --> 00:06:27,207
as the mountains.

78
00:06:27,208 --> 00:06:31,780
You see, they are also all working on compression, not under tension.

79
00:06:31,781 --> 00:06:35,720
working on compression is working like the pyramid

80
00:06:35,721 --> 00:06:38,800
and sometimes they even discover mortar

81
00:06:38,801 --> 00:06:40,860
mortar is a weak path

82
00:06:40,861 --> 00:06:43,620
so we will do bridges without mortar

83
00:06:43,621 --> 00:06:45,700
just putting stones against stone

84
00:06:45,701 --> 00:06:49,260
so it is also a very deep sense of purity

85
00:06:49,261 --> 00:06:53,500
and a very deep sense of the artistic achievement

86
00:06:53,501 --> 00:06:57,680
When Rome fell in the 5th century AD

87
00:06:57,681 --> 00:06:59,840
the art of bridge building fell with it

88
00:06:59,841 --> 00:07:04,840
Many centuries would pass before another great bridge project was even attempted.

89
00:07:04,841 --> 00:07:11,840
The legendary London Bridge across the Thames, built over 33 years at the end of the 12th century,

90
00:07:11,841 --> 00:07:13,973
was a glorious mess of a bridge.

91
00:07:13,974 --> 00:07:18,840
Clumsy and ugly by Roman standards, but a bold breakthrough for its time.

92
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:26,840
935 feet long, London Bridge clopped across the Thames with no less than 19 arches

93
00:07:26,841 --> 00:07:32,640
arches and massive piers that were half as wide as the arch spans. The piers were so

94
00:07:32,641 --> 00:07:37,120
thick that they created a dangerous whirlpool in the river, and going under the bridge by

95
00:07:37,121 --> 00:07:39,799
boat was called shooting the bridge.

96
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:44,040
Despite its drawbacks, this was a great and vital bridge,

97
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:49,397
crammed with vendors, fashionable apartments, even a gallows.

98
00:07:49,398 --> 00:07:51,520
And it was a workhorse, the

99
00:07:51,521 --> 00:07:54,822
only bridge over the Thames for 600 years.

100
00:07:54,823 --> 00:07:58,360
It was finally torn down and replaced in 1831

101
00:07:58,361 --> 00:08:01,455
by the heavily trafficked New London Bridge.

102
00:08:01,456 --> 00:08:04,760
That bridge didn't last nearly so long, sinking

103
00:08:04,761 --> 00:08:09,658
dangerously into the Thames and being dismantled in 1968.

104
00:08:09,659 --> 00:08:12,580
Bizarrely, the historic bridge was

105
00:08:12,581 --> 00:08:17,360
put up for sale and bought by an American who was building a resort town in Arizona

106
00:08:17,361 --> 00:08:18,960
called Lake Havasu.

107
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:22,380
He figured London Bridge would be a great asset.

108
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,940
And there she rests, a great bridge with a storied past.

109
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,460
The Renaissance brought the first real advances in bridge design since Rome,

110
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:37,880
as architects explored ways to elongate the arch.

111
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:43,440
Arches became continually more and more refined

112
00:08:43,441 --> 00:08:48,460
and more and more bold in their proportions.

113
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:54,000
An arch which is completely circular is very safe.

114
00:08:54,920 --> 00:08:58,900
And if you note, the Roman arches are indeed circular.

115
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:03,100
As you want to span longer distances,

116
00:09:03,580 --> 00:09:05,420
you open the arch more and more

117
00:09:05,421 --> 00:09:08,280
and the risk of having it break in the middle

118
00:09:08,281 --> 00:09:11,740
or kick out at the foot increases.

119
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:18,100
but gradually builders did become more and more audacious

120
00:09:18,101 --> 00:09:22,280
and arches did get a different proportion.

121
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:24,980
They became lower and longer gradually.

122
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:29,080
Many of the great bridges of Paris

123
00:09:29,081 --> 00:09:32,980
were built by a pioneering bridge designer named Jean-Rodolphe Peronet

124
00:09:32,981 --> 00:09:35,220
who perfected the Masonry Arch Bridge.

125
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:39,500
Peronet made a revolutionary discovery about arches.

126
00:09:40,140 --> 00:09:43,420
Their thrust is not entirely downward, but also lateral.

127
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:48,060
Peers, it turned out, could be much thinner than anyone thought,

128
00:09:48,660 --> 00:09:50,460
one-fifth the size of those of Rome,

129
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:54,240
so long as solid abutments picked up the stress on either side.

130
00:09:56,620 --> 00:09:59,260
Where you get more of an elliptical or flat arch,

131
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:01,520
you begin to develop thrust,

132
00:10:01,521 --> 00:10:05,060
in other words, the tendency of that arch to want to push out.

133
00:10:05,061 --> 00:10:08,520
and so you've got to come up with another solution

134
00:10:08,521 --> 00:10:10,200
restrain that thrust

135
00:10:10,201 --> 00:10:13,420
and the solution is massive abutments

136
00:10:13,421 --> 00:10:15,180
a massive amount of stone

137
00:10:15,181 --> 00:10:17,860
that's going to counteract the tendency of that arch

138
00:10:17,861 --> 00:10:20,420
to want to move out.

139
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:24,340
Pironet designed the Pont de la Concorde in Paris

140
00:10:24,341 --> 00:10:26,700
in 1791 when he was 82.

141
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:29,720
The bridge which stretches across the Seine

142
00:10:29,721 --> 00:10:31,220
in a few elegant strides

143
00:10:31,221 --> 00:10:33,220
is considered his masterpiece.

144
00:10:35,060 --> 00:10:42,700
Since ancient times, stone had been the only material one could use in building a major span.

145
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:47,920
At the end of the 18th century, all that was about to change.

146
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:53,680
Bridges have always had a spiritual connotation.

147
00:10:53,681 --> 00:11:00,460
The official Latin title for the Pope is the Pontifex Maximus, or the Supreme Builder of the Bridge.

148
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:04,540
Bridges will return on Modern Marvels.

149
00:11:05,060 --> 00:11:14,520
Until the end of the 18th century, bridges had been pretty much constructed of two materials, wood and stone.

150
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:24,040
In 1779, in the industrial midlands of England, a new material was introduced, cast iron.

151
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:31,460
Iron Bridge, as it was called, spanned the Severn River outside the tiny village of Colbrookdale,

152
00:11:31,461 --> 00:11:35,360
where it augured a revolution in bridge construction and design.

153
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:40,240
Small, quiet, delicate.

154
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:45,220
This is the forerunner of the iron and steel monsters of the 19th and 20th centuries.

155
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:49,100
Not just arch bridges, but suspension spans as well.

156
00:11:50,860 --> 00:11:55,220
A local foundry commissioned iron bridge is a way of showing off what cast iron,

157
00:11:55,221 --> 00:11:58,860
which was stronger, more flexible, and cheaper than stone could do.

158
00:11:58,861 --> 00:12:06,580
Ironically, though it pointed to the future, Iron Bridge was erected like a bridge from the past.

159
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:15,740
It is a beautifully made bridge, and what's interesting about it is if you look at the way they worked the iron, they worked the iron as though it were wood.

160
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:21,300
And the construction of the bridge is a mirror of wood construction.

161
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:24,720
It was as though they were just learning how to use the material.

162
00:12:24,721 --> 00:12:34,180
The bridge, which consists of five arching ribs and 800 pieces of iron, is held together like woodwork, without a single bolt.

163
00:12:35,140 --> 00:12:38,300
Instead, tongue and groove fittings and screws keep it in place.

164
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:43,700
At first, Iron Bridge was admired more as a novelty than a breakthrough.

165
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:48,880
But when it was the only bridge to survive a flood on the Severn, engineers took note.

166
00:12:48,881 --> 00:12:55,960
Indeed, the Manai Strait Bridge, built off the coast of Wales in 1826,

167
00:12:56,380 --> 00:13:01,520
used iron chains to hold up a record-setting 580-foot suspension span.

168
00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:05,880
Designed by renowned engineer Thomas Telford,

169
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:09,620
this was the world's first large-scale suspension bridge,

170
00:13:09,621 --> 00:13:15,180
a quantum leap that launched an era of suspension supremacy that continues to this day.

171
00:13:15,181 --> 00:13:20,120
The principle behind the suspension bridge is simple

172
00:13:20,121 --> 00:13:24,680
Cables anchored in an abutment on one end are strung over two towers

173
00:13:24,681 --> 00:13:27,640
And then anchored in an abutment on the other end

174
00:13:27,641 --> 00:13:34,100
The suspended cables support a roadway and deck by way of smaller vertical cables or suspenders

175
00:13:34,101 --> 00:13:39,640
Suspension bridges existed before iron, but on a very small scale

176
00:13:39,641 --> 00:13:42,920
With hemp and fibers used to hold up the bridge

177
00:13:45,180 --> 00:13:54,280
Telford fashioned 16 wrought iron I-bar chains made up of hundreds of small iron bars bolted together at either end, like a bicycle chain.

178
00:13:54,920 --> 00:14:00,840
He put the giant chains through hundreds of load tests to make sure they were strong enough to hold up the Mammoth Bridge.

179
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:12,420
1,710 feet long, counting the approaches, the Manai Strait was the longest bridge in the world in 1826, a first for a suspension span.

180
00:14:15,180 --> 00:14:18,740
While Europe was exploring the possibilities of iron and the suspension bridge,

181
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:23,020
America was throwing up thousands of covered truss bridges made of wood.

182
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:28,560
Wooden bridges flourished in the U.S. more than anywhere else in the world.

183
00:14:30,620 --> 00:14:32,100
Convenience was a major reason.

184
00:14:32,620 --> 00:14:35,800
With huge forest lands, wood was available and cheap.

185
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:43,560
Also, a strong, highly creative woodworking tradition produced literally hundreds of truss designs.

186
00:14:45,180 --> 00:14:49,220
A truss bridge is one that uses its frame to help prop itself up.

187
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:54,800
A frame based on the power of the triangle, the strongest geometric shape around.

188
00:14:55,760 --> 00:15:00,280
The basic concept of a truss bridge is a three-sided triangle.

189
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:09,980
And the phenomenon about the three-sided triangle is it's the only form out of a rectangle, a trapezoid, a circle,

190
00:15:10,440 --> 00:15:14,200
that will not change shape if you put a force on it.

191
00:15:15,180 --> 00:15:19,620
So a series of rigid triangles arranged in some sort of configuration,

192
00:15:19,980 --> 00:15:24,060
which is what differentiated between the different truss types,

193
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:28,240
is the fundamental principle of the truss.

194
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:33,220
Most of the bridges were covered to protect them from the elements.

195
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:40,780
Toll keepers often had to shovel snow onto these so-called kissing bridges to accommodate sleighs.

196
00:15:42,020 --> 00:15:44,020
The railroads put an end to the wooden bridge.

197
00:15:44,600 --> 00:15:49,060
trains were simply too heavy and threw off sparks that set many bridges on fire.

198
00:15:50,320 --> 00:15:54,320
For the 1840s, metal truss bridges were sweeping the country,

199
00:15:55,060 --> 00:16:00,100
bridges that literally came in a box and could be assembled by a crew of farmers in a few days.

200
00:16:01,660 --> 00:16:07,100
These bridges were usually fabricated in the eastern industrial centers,

201
00:16:07,880 --> 00:16:13,020
such as Pittsburgh, where a small factory would fabricate a bridge.

202
00:16:13,021 --> 00:16:19,880
It would be laid out on the factory floor, pre-assembled to make sure that all the pieces went together,

203
00:16:20,740 --> 00:16:28,480
then broken apart, loaded on a railroad, shipped 150, 250 miles across the country,

204
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,240
and then be re-erected in the middle of nowhere.

205
00:16:33,640 --> 00:16:37,160
The race to come up with the perfect truss design intensified.

206
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:40,580
800 truss patents were issued overall.

207
00:16:40,581 --> 00:16:47,700
Scores of bridge factories competed to build up the small town infrastructure of America,

208
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:53,380
erecting tens of thousands of prefabricated metal bridges by century's end.

209
00:16:57,580 --> 00:17:03,960
The great American achievement in 19th century bridge building was not, however, the prefabricated metal truss.

210
00:17:04,520 --> 00:17:08,340
The Civil War jump-started the country's iron and steel industries,

211
00:17:08,341 --> 00:17:13,440
and bridge builders turned their attention to the exciting possibilities of the suspension bridge.

212
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:18,980
John Roebling, a German immigrant engineer, came up with a new kind of suspension cable

213
00:17:18,981 --> 00:17:21,920
that would revolutionize suspension spans.

214
00:17:22,980 --> 00:17:28,560
Roebling's great breakthrough was the use of parallel wires, pencil-thin wires,

215
00:17:29,300 --> 00:17:33,700
to gather, pack together, providing a strength that would be greater

216
00:17:33,701 --> 00:17:38,760
and a flexibility that would be greater than a steel beam or an iron beam.

217
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:44,500
So whereas in the past you might have had an I-bar or bar going from location to location,

218
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:49,180
now you would have a series of strands of steel wire.

219
00:17:49,181 --> 00:17:56,720
And the steel wire together is A, as strong or stronger, B, as flexible or it's more flexible,

220
00:17:57,280 --> 00:18:00,500
and C, allow for some breakage without a failure.

221
00:18:00,501 --> 00:18:07,240
Roebling's iron rope allowed the suspension bridge to span unheard of lengths.

222
00:18:08,260 --> 00:18:13,660
In 1869 he began work on a bridge over New York's East River between Brooklyn and Manhattan.

223
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:19,860
A 1600 foot span, 500 feet longer than the longest suspension bridge in the world.

224
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:21,940
Impossible, the experts agreed.

225
00:18:21,941 --> 00:18:29,440
One of the great British engineers wrote to Roebling that his bridge is insane and impossible,

226
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:34,140
but should it be proven possible, everybody else would look stupid.

227
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:39,820
And it definitely is possible. It has been there for 115 years now.

228
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:46,900
It took 14 years and more than 20 lives, including Roebling's, to build the Brooklyn Bridge.

229
00:18:47,440 --> 00:18:48,600
But build it he did.

230
00:18:48,601 --> 00:18:53,000
The Brooklyn Bridge is the first truly modern bridge.

231
00:18:53,620 --> 00:18:59,800
In both construction and design, this was the prototype for the great suspension landmarks of the 20th century.

232
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:05,960
Groobling pioneered the process and the technique for building a bridge on such a scale.

233
00:19:06,660 --> 00:19:11,700
The use of pneumatic caissons to dredge out the riverbed and lay massive foundations.

234
00:19:12,740 --> 00:19:18,520
The endless, painstaking spinning of cables, building them up wire by wire.

235
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:26,020
5,434 wires per strand, 19 strands per cable, four cables in all.

236
00:19:27,080 --> 00:19:32,480
Most importantly, at a time when many suspension bridges were collapsing from heavy traffic and wind,

237
00:19:33,240 --> 00:19:36,780
Roebling figured out a way to stabilize a giant suspension span,

238
00:19:37,420 --> 00:19:42,560
designing a triple system of supports that could withstand every kind of stress.

239
00:19:42,561 --> 00:19:48,000
and Roebling invented a structural scheme

240
00:19:48,001 --> 00:19:51,720
which he was confident, correctly so,

241
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:53,940
would resist any wind.

242
00:19:55,080 --> 00:19:58,180
This scheme consists of the suspension system

243
00:19:58,181 --> 00:20:03,640
which in turn has the cables and the suspenders.

244
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:07,560
That alone is enough to keep the bridge up.

245
00:20:08,260 --> 00:20:10,600
But also there are these diagonal stays

246
00:20:10,601 --> 00:20:15,260
fanning out from the top of the tower towards the roadway.

247
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:20,440
And then he has a quite substantial stiffening truss going alongside the bridge.

248
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:26,680
Roebling argued if any one of these three systems should fail,

249
00:20:27,480 --> 00:20:30,840
the other two will sustain the bridge and prevent the collapse.

250
00:20:32,040 --> 00:20:35,460
As it happens, one of the systems did fail.

251
00:20:35,461 --> 00:20:39,180
The diagonal stays failed due to corrosion.

252
00:20:39,181 --> 00:20:42,360
They corroded so much that one fell down.

253
00:20:43,340 --> 00:20:47,700
Owing to the large degree of redundancy that he built into his bridge,

254
00:20:48,120 --> 00:20:52,120
it was not a problem replacing any one component at the time.

255
00:20:53,960 --> 00:20:57,340
Roebling may have launched the modern era of monumental spans,

256
00:20:57,780 --> 00:21:03,300
but the coming century would soon produce bridges that would make the Brooklyn Bridge seem small.

257
00:21:03,301 --> 00:21:09,720
There are many great bridge cities in the world.

258
00:21:10,440 --> 00:21:11,400
Paris, for example.

259
00:21:12,160 --> 00:21:13,060
Or New York.

260
00:21:14,300 --> 00:21:15,900
Or San Francisco.

261
00:21:17,100 --> 00:21:24,520
From an engineering point of view, none of the great Bay Area bridges is more impressive than the San Francisco-Oakland Bay Bridge.

262
00:21:26,340 --> 00:21:31,940
The workhorse bridge of the region, more than 100 million vehicles a year cross the Bay Bridge.

263
00:21:31,941 --> 00:21:34,480
280,000 a day

264
00:21:34,481 --> 00:21:36,780
more than any other bridge in the world

265
00:21:36,781 --> 00:21:40,620
Built during the depression of the 1930s

266
00:21:40,621 --> 00:21:42,340
and opened in 1936

267
00:21:42,341 --> 00:21:44,100
this hybrid bridge

268
00:21:44,101 --> 00:21:47,480
part suspension, part cantilever, part truss

269
00:21:47,481 --> 00:21:50,120
stretches an incredible 8.5 miles

270
00:21:50,121 --> 00:21:53,380
over the deep and difficult waters of the San Francisco Bay

271
00:21:53,381 --> 00:21:57,620
Everybody thinks of the Golden Gate Bridge

272
00:21:57,621 --> 00:21:58,840
when they go to San Francisco

273
00:21:58,841 --> 00:22:00,880
I think about the Oakland Bay Bridge

274
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:04,320
I think that's even nicer than the Golden Gate.

275
00:22:04,820 --> 00:22:09,480
It's almost magical in the sense that it's two suspension bridges,

276
00:22:09,820 --> 00:22:14,460
and I wonder how many people notice the anchorage that's built right into the water there.

277
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:18,380
And so you think that that's a suspension bridge that goes on forever,

278
00:22:18,381 --> 00:22:22,900
but it really is just two separate suspension bridges joined together

279
00:22:22,901 --> 00:22:29,120
and creating this elegant catenary that seems to go on for miles and miles.

280
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:33,600
The Bay Bridge design is unique.

281
00:22:34,240 --> 00:22:38,440
A double suspension bridge, each half of which is bigger than the Brooklyn Bridge,

282
00:22:38,720 --> 00:22:42,100
runs from San Francisco to Yerba Buena Island in the middle of the bay.

283
00:22:43,100 --> 00:22:48,480
A double deck tunnel carries traffic through the island and onto the eastern half of the bridge,

284
00:22:48,860 --> 00:22:52,000
which consists of one of the world's largest cantilever spans

285
00:22:52,001 --> 00:22:55,700
and a long series of trusses that extend to the Oakland shore.

286
00:22:55,701 --> 00:23:02,660
The most remarkable part of this bridge, as is often the case, is the part you can't see

287
00:23:02,661 --> 00:23:05,020
The underwater anchorages and piers

288
00:23:05,021 --> 00:23:12,200
Massive piers had to be founded at record depths, as much as 240 feet to bedrock on the eastern side

289
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:19,480
An artificial island, meanwhile, had to be built in the middle of the bay to anchor the two suspension spans

290
00:23:19,481 --> 00:23:23,400
This central anchorage was made of pure concrete

291
00:23:23,401 --> 00:23:25,900
more than went into the Empire State Building.

292
00:23:26,840 --> 00:23:32,220
People may think an anchorage is simply a block that just stands there,

293
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:34,100
but it's got an enormous function.

294
00:23:34,260 --> 00:23:38,660
It has the entire responsibility of holding up that bridge.

295
00:23:39,140 --> 00:23:40,700
Now imagine in the Bay Bridge,

296
00:23:41,080 --> 00:23:46,340
you've got the forces of the first bridge trying to pull it up this way

297
00:23:46,341 --> 00:23:51,080
and the forces of the other bridge trying to pull it up in the opposite direction

298
00:23:51,081 --> 00:23:56,460
and you're trying to be right in the center of a waterway

299
00:23:56,461 --> 00:23:59,600
in which you've got some harsh currents and harsh conditions.

300
00:24:00,120 --> 00:24:02,340
That's a magnificent concept.

301
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:05,100
Just dreaming it up took a lot of courage.

302
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:11,240
520-foot towers, the size of 60-story high-rises,

303
00:24:11,241 --> 00:24:13,940
and the largest structures in the Bay Area skyline

304
00:24:13,941 --> 00:24:18,720
consumed 18% of all the steel produced in the U.S. in 1934.

305
00:24:21,080 --> 00:24:27,480
70,000 miles worth of pencil-thin wire went into the cables that hold up this extraordinary span.

306
00:24:30,820 --> 00:24:36,140
As an engineering triumph, the Bay Bridge was hailed as the greatest bridge of its time.

307
00:24:38,480 --> 00:24:43,860
Only a year after it opened in 1936, however, another bridge stole the spotlight.

308
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:48,460
A bridge located just to the north, across the waters of the Golden Gate Strait.

309
00:24:49,700 --> 00:24:50,920
The Golden Gate.

310
00:24:51,080 --> 00:24:55,020
Perhaps the most famous, most beautiful suspension bridge in the world.

311
00:24:56,620 --> 00:25:01,120
And it was world champion in length until Verrazano came along.

312
00:25:01,920 --> 00:25:03,700
And that was in 1964.

313
00:25:04,780 --> 00:25:06,180
It's a magnificent bridge.

314
00:25:06,960 --> 00:25:10,380
Obviously the surroundings, which are also magnificent, help.

315
00:25:11,060 --> 00:25:16,900
But it's an extremely beautiful masterpiece of a structure.

316
00:25:16,901 --> 00:25:22,900
A masterpiece that took tremendous determination and will to complete.

317
00:25:23,820 --> 00:25:28,440
The waters of the Golden Gate were even more turbulent than those of the San Francisco Bay.

318
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:37,580
A 1,000-foot trestle, or temporary roadway that carried materials from San Francisco to the South Pier, was destroyed by a storm.

319
00:25:38,680 --> 00:25:44,260
After five months of repairs, it was destroyed again, this time by a ship lost in the fog.

320
00:25:45,020 --> 00:25:49,380
A year into construction, engineers had to come up with another way of building the pier.

321
00:25:50,720 --> 00:25:56,480
But when it was finished, the Golden Gate, with its 4,200-foot central span,

322
00:25:57,080 --> 00:26:00,920
famous Art Deco towers, and striking paint job,

323
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:05,260
was immediately hailed as a triumph of engineering and art.

324
00:26:06,140 --> 00:26:09,800
A color called International Orange was used to paint this bridge

325
00:26:09,801 --> 00:26:12,560
because of the way it complements the Golden Gate straight.

326
00:26:12,561 --> 00:26:22,060
If you take the Golden Gate in San Francisco, you will see a symbiosis or a synergy between bridge and place.

327
00:26:22,780 --> 00:26:29,220
I mean, the bridge is so beautiful that it makes this place even more beautiful.

328
00:26:30,320 --> 00:26:34,140
And the place is also so beautiful that makes the bridge completely unique.

329
00:26:35,520 --> 00:26:40,200
The Golden Gate was the longest suspension bridge in the world until 1964,

330
00:26:40,201 --> 00:26:44,320
when the Verrazano Narrows in New York passed it by 60 feet.

331
00:26:45,300 --> 00:26:48,600
The Verrazano is still the longest suspension span in the U.S.

332
00:26:50,740 --> 00:26:53,700
But size isn't everything when it comes to evaluating a bridge.

333
00:26:54,280 --> 00:26:59,520
The vast majority of the 550,000 bridges in the United States are relatively small.

334
00:26:59,720 --> 00:27:06,160
A small bridge with a long lifespan is just as much an achievement

335
00:27:06,161 --> 00:27:09,920
as a short-lived bridge with a long physical span.

336
00:27:10,200 --> 00:27:19,340
Some of the most notable small bridges in America are in Central Park, which boasts no less than 38 spans, none of them alike.

337
00:27:19,900 --> 00:27:22,780
All of them carefully designed to blend in with the park.

338
00:27:24,900 --> 00:27:29,220
Huddleston Bridge is made from rocks that were excavated while digging nearby roads.

339
00:27:30,080 --> 00:27:35,880
The stones were laid dry, without mortar, and create an arch that seems to have tumbled into place.

340
00:27:35,881 --> 00:27:45,000
Goldarch Bridge is one of five iron bridges in Central Park, which has half the iron bridges in the U.S.

341
00:27:46,800 --> 00:27:52,900
Built between 1859 and 1861, these graceful Gothic spans are beautifully detailed

342
00:27:52,901 --> 00:27:57,160
and help maintain the escape-from-the-city ambience of the park.

343
00:27:58,900 --> 00:28:03,440
In fact, the bridges of Central Park play a crucial role in protecting that ambience.

344
00:28:05,880 --> 00:28:13,200
Camouflaged overpasses, like this one, separate people going through the park from cars and buses below.

345
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:20,880
They sunk those roads below the grade of the park so that traffic actually moves across the park,

346
00:28:21,180 --> 00:28:25,360
but you never know it. You walk over the bridge and you walk across it.

347
00:28:25,940 --> 00:28:30,540
And if they were at grade and you had stoplights and everything,

348
00:28:30,541 --> 00:28:40,380
Rather than having one big park of 843 acres, you really would probably end up with five smaller parks just because of the traffic flow.

349
00:28:42,440 --> 00:28:45,680
Bridges don't just separate lines of traffic on land.

350
00:28:46,380 --> 00:28:53,260
A special category of bridges makes it possible for low-hanging river spans to carry vehicular traffic without blocking boats.

351
00:28:53,980 --> 00:28:56,480
Coming up, bridges that move.

352
00:29:00,540 --> 00:29:06,480
The Chinese built the world's first iron chain-link suspension bridge in the 13th century.

353
00:29:07,560 --> 00:29:10,200
Bridges will return on Modern Marvels.

354
00:29:12,780 --> 00:29:16,640
Anyone interested in learning about bridges would do well to visit Pittsburgh,

355
00:29:17,200 --> 00:29:21,520
a city with more bridges per capita than any place in the world except Venice, Italy.

356
00:29:22,860 --> 00:29:27,040
Pittsburgh doesn't have hundreds of canals, but it does have three rivers.

357
00:29:27,660 --> 00:29:29,800
The Monongahela, Allegheny, and Ohio.

358
00:29:30,540 --> 00:29:34,520
which at least partly explains why more than 1,700 bridges are needed

359
00:29:34,521 --> 00:29:37,300
to help people get around the city and its environs.

360
00:29:38,220 --> 00:29:42,380
Pittsburgh is like a miniature model of Manhattan itself,

361
00:29:42,900 --> 00:29:45,860
where everything is reduced except the number of bridges,

362
00:29:46,280 --> 00:29:50,100
which has not only not been reduced, but actually been increased.

363
00:29:50,500 --> 00:29:55,000
They have arches, they have trusses of all kinds.

364
00:29:55,001 --> 00:30:00,940
it's like kids let loose to build any kind of bridge they would possibly like.

365
00:30:02,140 --> 00:30:05,920
And they build new ones, they maintain the old ones.

366
00:30:06,040 --> 00:30:09,440
It's an exciting place, particularly for steel bridges.

367
00:30:11,020 --> 00:30:14,640
Pittsburgh, of course, was the steel capital of the world for much of the century.

368
00:30:15,540 --> 00:30:19,980
And what better way to use and showcase huge amounts of steel than in a bridge?

369
00:30:20,660 --> 00:30:24,640
What you have in Pittsburgh is you have a lot of really beautiful bridges.

370
00:30:24,641 --> 00:30:29,240
bridges that were designed to be beautiful, that were designed to make a statement,

371
00:30:29,400 --> 00:30:33,560
that were designed to say, we're Pittsburgh, we are the industrial center of America,

372
00:30:33,900 --> 00:30:36,820
we're important, look at these beautiful things we can build.

373
00:30:38,180 --> 00:30:43,360
Three of the most beautiful bridges in Pittsburgh are the 6th, 7th, and 9th Street bridges.

374
00:30:43,980 --> 00:30:45,560
The Three Sisters, as they're called.

375
00:30:46,180 --> 00:30:48,720
Identical suspension spans lined up in a row.

376
00:30:50,360 --> 00:30:53,240
What makes these bridges unusual beyond their configuration

377
00:30:53,241 --> 00:30:57,180
is the fact that they are old-fashioned I-bar suspension bridges,

378
00:30:57,820 --> 00:31:00,700
held in place by steel chains rather than wire cables.

379
00:31:02,320 --> 00:31:04,120
And that they are self-anchored.

380
00:31:04,580 --> 00:31:07,840
That is, they aren't held in place by huge anchorages on each side.

381
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:11,360
Instead, the suspension cables are tied to the deck itself,

382
00:31:11,540 --> 00:31:13,000
which holds the structure in place.

383
00:31:14,980 --> 00:31:18,020
Another impressive rarity is the Smithfield Bridge,

384
00:31:18,580 --> 00:31:19,660
the city's oldest.

385
00:31:19,661 --> 00:31:23,480
designed and built in 1883 by Gustav Lindenthal,

386
00:31:23,900 --> 00:31:26,340
a legendary bridge engineer of the last century.

387
00:31:27,880 --> 00:31:31,920
Called a lenticular truss because of its parabolic or lens-like design,

388
00:31:32,640 --> 00:31:36,140
this is the last double-spam bridge of its kind in the United States.

389
00:31:38,540 --> 00:31:42,400
This slender concrete giant, the Westinghouse Bridge,

390
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:46,160
was the longest and highest concrete arch highway bridge in the world

391
00:31:46,161 --> 00:31:48,220
when it was built in 1932.

392
00:31:49,660 --> 00:31:53,160
It crosses over an industrial valley, the Turtle Creek Valley,

393
00:31:53,420 --> 00:31:58,820
and the architects and engineers said that they wanted to bring order to the chaos of the valley,

394
00:31:59,140 --> 00:32:01,700
which was already crossed by three layers of transportation,

395
00:32:02,080 --> 00:32:05,140
and there's two big mills down there, and there's a stream down there.

396
00:32:05,141 --> 00:32:12,120
And then just rising up above this valley is this marvelous, slender bridge,

397
00:32:12,760 --> 00:32:15,460
and it's such a contrast to the rest of the valley,

398
00:32:15,700 --> 00:32:17,780
and that's the goal they were striving for.

399
00:32:17,781 --> 00:32:20,420
They wanted beauty, not just functionality at that bridge.

400
00:32:20,720 --> 00:32:23,780
I think it's the loveliest bridge in Allegheny County.

401
00:32:24,780 --> 00:32:28,140
The American Bridge Company, an offshoot of U.S. steel,

402
00:32:28,700 --> 00:32:31,340
fabricated the steel for all of Pittsburgh's bridges,

403
00:32:31,860 --> 00:32:34,360
along with thousands of other bridges around the world.

404
00:32:35,820 --> 00:32:40,980
The bridge building operation was so huge that an entire town grew up around it.

405
00:32:41,460 --> 00:32:43,640
Ambridge, short for American Bridge.

406
00:32:43,641 --> 00:32:55,090
All told, these fabrication workshops, which were shut down in the 1980s, churned out steel parts for over 10,000 bridges, including the San Francisco-Oakland Bay

407
00:32:55,091 --> 00:32:59,260
Bridge, the Mackinac Bridge, and many other landmark spans.

408
00:33:00,880 --> 00:33:07,920
But for all her bridges, there is one species of bridge that Pittsburgh does not have, the movable bridge.

409
00:33:09,560 --> 00:33:11,240
The movable bridge.

410
00:33:12,060 --> 00:33:15,820
As a rule, the one thing we want our bridges not to do is move.

411
00:33:17,120 --> 00:33:18,480
But there are exceptions.

412
00:33:19,280 --> 00:33:25,320
A whole category of bridges that work like giant machines regulating conflicting lines of land and water traffic.

413
00:33:26,120 --> 00:33:28,700
We have a wide variety of movable bridges.

414
00:33:28,980 --> 00:33:30,120
I think they're the most fun.

415
00:33:30,800 --> 00:33:37,600
It's the problem of how do you get a ship going through, how do you get the traffic over, how do you solve that problem.

416
00:33:37,601 --> 00:33:42,120
and there are a half dozen different ways people have solved that problem.

417
00:33:42,780 --> 00:33:48,980
Well, maybe you swing the bridge on an axle and the bridge just rotates and then boats can go by.

418
00:33:49,560 --> 00:33:50,260
That's one way.

419
00:33:51,520 --> 00:33:59,740
Maybe the bridge opens like a drawbridge and, you know, you have a single leaf drawbridge or a double leaf drawbridge.

420
00:34:00,740 --> 00:34:03,760
Maybe you could have a bridge just lift up vertically.

421
00:34:03,761 --> 00:34:05,580
I mean, isn't that a great concept?

422
00:34:05,700 --> 00:34:07,780
Somebody thinks that the bridge will just go vertically.

423
00:34:07,920 --> 00:34:12,020
Those are lift bridges and give you the widest possible way for ships to pass,

424
00:34:12,120 --> 00:34:14,260
but don't give you the height.

425
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:19,180
There's another bridge that's very rare, although we're lucky we have one in New York City,

426
00:34:19,660 --> 00:34:21,000
and that's a retractable bridge.

427
00:34:21,480 --> 00:34:23,980
And that kind of combines a bridge with railroads.

428
00:34:24,080 --> 00:34:25,860
The bridge becomes a railroad.

429
00:34:26,080 --> 00:34:29,800
It's on rail lines and slides out of the way.

430
00:34:29,801 --> 00:34:36,020
and the Carroll Street Bridge is a beautiful example of it.

431
00:34:38,720 --> 00:34:41,340
Moveable bridges are usually located in cities

432
00:34:41,341 --> 00:34:44,640
where land is too expensive to build along approach roads

433
00:34:44,641 --> 00:34:46,660
needed for high-level fixed bridges.

434
00:34:48,520 --> 00:34:51,280
Trains, which can only rise at a 1% grade,

435
00:34:51,780 --> 00:34:54,720
need 5,000 feet to ease on to a fixed bridge

436
00:34:54,721 --> 00:34:56,360
that's just 50 feet high.

437
00:34:56,361 --> 00:35:09,049
One of the most celebrated and largest movable bridges in the world is the Tower Bridge of London, a bascule or drawbridge type bridge with a 1,045 ton roadway that

438
00:35:09,050 --> 00:35:12,840
opens up to let traffic on the Thames River pass.

439
00:35:14,160 --> 00:35:20,840
This majestic bridge was built in the 1890s across the Pool of London, the busiest port in the world at that time.

440
00:35:20,841 --> 00:35:26,620
It gave millions of East Londoners a desperately needed way of getting across the Thames

441
00:35:26,621 --> 00:35:31,480
without in any way hindering the hundreds of ships that passed in and out of the pool each day.

442
00:35:32,780 --> 00:35:37,780
A triumph of Victorian engineering, the bridge was powered by enormous steam engines,

443
00:35:38,200 --> 00:35:43,740
the largest in the world at one time, which opened and closed the massive decks in less than a minute.

444
00:35:46,680 --> 00:35:50,100
Impatient pedestrians can still use a unique bridge within a bridge,

445
00:35:50,101 --> 00:35:54,400
connecting the towers at the top to cross over while the bascules are up.

446
00:35:56,320 --> 00:35:58,380
Located adjacent to the Tower of London,

447
00:35:58,940 --> 00:36:02,940
Tower Bridge is wrapped up in masonry and detailed like a medieval tower

448
00:36:02,941 --> 00:36:05,100
to fit in with its historic surroundings.

449
00:36:06,020 --> 00:36:09,120
In fact, this is one of the first great modern steel bridges

450
00:36:09,121 --> 00:36:11,200
made to look like an antique.

451
00:36:11,340 --> 00:36:15,220
The effect of this architectural costume is magical.

452
00:36:16,440 --> 00:36:19,000
It happens once you add a stone to it.

453
00:36:19,001 --> 00:36:21,500
You see, the stone is a support to your imagination.

454
00:36:21,860 --> 00:36:25,000
And then you can transport even your dreams, you know, to that.

455
00:36:25,080 --> 00:36:28,440
I'm thinking it is more than a bridge, it's a castle or something like that.

456
00:36:28,480 --> 00:36:29,860
You understand? And it is a fortress.

457
00:36:30,780 --> 00:36:34,260
And it works so well with the whole surrounding, with the city of London,

458
00:36:34,360 --> 00:36:35,880
with the character of the city of London,

459
00:36:36,260 --> 00:36:39,000
that the bridge itself is such a landmark to London

460
00:36:39,001 --> 00:36:40,940
as much as the houses of the parliament.

461
00:36:42,280 --> 00:36:45,800
I would love to go over to London and operate the Tower Bridge.

462
00:36:45,801 --> 00:36:48,960
I mean, that is such a huge bridge and just feel.

463
00:36:49,000 --> 00:36:51,260
What it feels like to operate a bridge that large.

464
00:36:51,820 --> 00:36:54,580
You know, that must be really something for that guy when he operates that bridge.

465
00:36:57,120 --> 00:36:59,920
Moveable bridges, of course, aren't always so large.

466
00:37:00,780 --> 00:37:05,400
This bascule across the Gowanus Canal in the Bronx is barely 100 feet long,

467
00:37:05,940 --> 00:37:09,700
which makes it a giant compared to this one-man swing bridge,

468
00:37:09,940 --> 00:37:12,840
which opens up to let the occasional boat pass by.

469
00:37:12,841 --> 00:37:20,040
For all of their diversity, the potential range and structure of bridges has barely been tapped.

470
00:37:20,820 --> 00:37:26,280
The 21st century promises to bring an unprecedented imaginative quality to bridge design.

471
00:37:31,120 --> 00:37:38,080
When bridge experts talk about the future of bridges, one name keeps popping up, Santiago Calatrava.

472
00:37:38,081 --> 00:37:44,380
Who is Santiago Calatrava? We're all watching anxiously to find out.

473
00:37:45,560 --> 00:37:49,660
Santiago Calatrava is a Spanish bridge designer living in Zurich,

474
00:37:50,320 --> 00:37:54,920
who at the age of 48 has already designed over 50 bridges worldwide.

475
00:37:56,160 --> 00:38:00,040
Startling, original bridges, unlike any that have come before.

476
00:38:00,041 --> 00:38:11,460
He is an extremely talented man who has stated that to him it's only one art, the art of building.

477
00:38:12,460 --> 00:38:19,640
And he employs his talents in building a bridge or a building or children's toys, as the case may be.

478
00:38:20,560 --> 00:38:27,160
And he does not comprehend the division between architects and engineers, particularly when applied to himself.

479
00:38:27,161 --> 00:38:34,200
Unlike many contemporary engineers, Calatravasi's bridges is more than mere infrastructure.

480
00:38:34,201 --> 00:38:40,040
What I discovered in the bridges, it is their capacity to dignify a place, a landscape.

481
00:38:40,920 --> 00:38:49,120
And I think it is essential, you know, to use the element of a bridge as much as you can use in a city the element of a cathedral

482
00:38:49,121 --> 00:38:53,980
or the element of a particular sculpture or a particular work of art,

483
00:38:54,060 --> 00:38:57,240
you know, who will dignify the life of the surrounding,

484
00:38:57,400 --> 00:38:59,260
who will dignify the image of the city,

485
00:38:59,400 --> 00:39:02,120
who will dignify and contribute to the people.

486
00:39:03,440 --> 00:39:06,740
Calatrava bridges, like this slender beauty outside Paris,

487
00:39:07,260 --> 00:39:09,360
appeal to the imagination and the eye.

488
00:39:11,420 --> 00:39:13,940
His bridges are unusually suggestive,

489
00:39:13,941 --> 00:39:16,500
and one of his favorite metaphors is the anatomy,

490
00:39:17,180 --> 00:39:20,260
especially the skeleton, which is evident in many of his designs.

491
00:39:21,680 --> 00:39:23,680
Once you go into the bridge itself,

492
00:39:23,800 --> 00:39:28,080
you discover that still there is a very wide march of expression in a bridge.

493
00:39:28,840 --> 00:39:31,360
This expression, in my case, in my particular case,

494
00:39:31,440 --> 00:39:33,860
is very much related to the structural nature,

495
00:39:33,861 --> 00:39:38,720
and so I try always to signify relations to bones

496
00:39:38,721 --> 00:39:43,200
or relation to a skelet or to the spine of fishes.

497
00:39:43,201 --> 00:39:48,880
The Alameo Bridge in Seville, Spain is one of Calatrava's most innovative.

498
00:39:49,940 --> 00:39:56,760
Normally, a cable-stayed bridge of this type is held in place by cables radiating from both sides of a vertical mast.

499
00:39:58,140 --> 00:40:02,080
Calatrava discovered that by leaning the mast and building up its weight,

500
00:40:02,660 --> 00:40:08,640
he could do away with the cables on one side, creating a strikingly suggestive minimalist span.

501
00:40:08,641 --> 00:40:17,520
You can say it is a sailboat, it is a harp, you see, to do music, a gigantic harp, you

502
00:40:17,521 --> 00:40:22,220
know, you can even think, you know, it is like a bird, you know, who goes up into the

503
00:40:22,221 --> 00:40:23,220
sky.

504
00:40:23,221 --> 00:40:26,520
We cannot stop our imagination to associate images.

505
00:40:26,521 --> 00:40:32,840
However, I would like very much to underline the steps who brought me to the Alamedo was

506
00:40:32,841 --> 00:40:38,180
very much related to the idea of making a bridge, you know, who is even more essential

507
00:40:38,181 --> 00:40:41,480
than the most essential cable-stair bridges built until this moment.

508
00:40:42,740 --> 00:40:44,400
To do bridges is almost a secret.

509
00:40:44,880 --> 00:40:47,640
It is a secret because it takes a lot of years

510
00:40:47,641 --> 00:40:50,900
until you get the essence of the statics,

511
00:40:51,140 --> 00:40:54,180
until you get the essence of the behavior of the bridge.

512
00:40:54,460 --> 00:40:57,440
And the more you study them, the more you respect them.

513
00:40:57,441 --> 00:40:59,520
You respect them in two terms.

514
00:40:59,720 --> 00:41:02,620
You respect them because it is difficult to do bridges

515
00:41:02,621 --> 00:41:04,620
and because it is beautiful to do bridges,

516
00:41:04,621 --> 00:41:08,600
but you respect also them because it is dangerous to do bridges.

517
00:41:09,820 --> 00:41:13,560
And so I think it is certainly, you know, it's an art.

518
00:41:13,980 --> 00:41:15,340
My opinion is a high art.

519
00:41:15,440 --> 00:41:19,440
It's a difficult one that you cannot improve from one day to another.

520
00:41:19,660 --> 00:41:20,960
You need almost a whole life.

521
00:41:23,300 --> 00:41:26,120
Kalitrava's disciplined yet audacious approach to bridges

522
00:41:26,121 --> 00:41:28,340
will soon be in evidence in the United States.

523
00:41:29,100 --> 00:41:32,620
In Milwaukee, Wisconsin, he is currently building a suspended footbridge

524
00:41:32,621 --> 00:41:39,620
footbridge with a two hundred foot angled mast for the Milwaukee Art Museum.

525
00:41:39,621 --> 00:41:44,320
And to revive downtown Dallas, he is convinced the city to fill the dry bed of the Trinity

526
00:41:44,321 --> 00:41:50,120
River with water and then decorate it with a series of water bridges.

527
00:41:50,121 --> 00:41:54,820
While Kalatrava searches for new ways to shape a bridge, the centuries old march to build

528
00:41:54,821 --> 00:42:00,420
the biggest, longest, widest bridge in the world goes on.

529
00:42:00,421 --> 00:42:04,680
The Japanese, who launched the greatest bridge-building program in history in 1970,

530
00:42:05,340 --> 00:42:09,780
a $27 billion project to link their islands with 18 major spans,

531
00:42:10,320 --> 00:42:13,200
now boast the world's longest suspension bridge.

532
00:42:14,740 --> 00:42:18,660
The Akashikaikyo Bridge, linking Kobe and Awaji Island,

533
00:42:19,280 --> 00:42:24,080
has a once inconceivable 6,527-foot span.

534
00:42:24,260 --> 00:42:26,660
That's four times the length of the Brooklyn Bridge,

535
00:42:26,920 --> 00:42:28,940
and a third longer than the Golden Gate.

536
00:42:29,900 --> 00:42:31,460
Where will it all end?

537
00:42:32,460 --> 00:42:35,660
Have we come close to reaching a limit on our ability to span?

538
00:42:36,660 --> 00:42:38,620
In a word, no.

539
00:42:39,560 --> 00:42:43,180
New materials, super light, super strong plastic fibers,

540
00:42:43,580 --> 00:42:45,980
will make seemingly impossible bridges viable.

541
00:42:47,140 --> 00:42:50,280
Already there's been serious talk of throwing a suspension bridge

542
00:42:50,281 --> 00:42:52,440
across the 23-mile English Channel.

543
00:42:54,440 --> 00:42:58,640
And of connecting Sicily to the Italian mainland across the Messina Strait.

544
00:42:58,940 --> 00:43:06,580
Before getting carried away imagining super spans of the future, however,

545
00:43:07,160 --> 00:43:10,020
we'd better learn to take care of the bridges we already have.

546
00:43:11,200 --> 00:43:16,080
Bridge maintenance rather than construction is the big challenge facing bridges today.

547
00:43:17,400 --> 00:43:23,020
Budget cuts and neglect have caused the collapse of scores of spans and the decay of thousands more.

548
00:43:23,021 --> 00:43:29,520
In the 1970s, no less than 40% of the bridges in America were deemed structurally deficient.

549
00:43:30,120 --> 00:43:36,880
A major mistake people have is they think a bridge is just there, it's physical, and needs very little attention.

550
00:43:37,800 --> 00:43:42,040
In fact, the bridge is moving. It's moving. It could be swaying back and forth.

551
00:43:42,120 --> 00:43:44,620
It could be rising and falling with the load.

552
00:43:44,740 --> 00:43:48,480
It could be expanding and contracting based on temperature.

553
00:43:49,100 --> 00:43:51,960
And as a result, it has to have lots of movable parts.

554
00:43:51,961 --> 00:43:56,400
all those pots have to be lubricated, they have to be kept clean.

555
00:43:56,920 --> 00:44:00,860
This is a machine, and if you don't take care of it,

556
00:44:00,920 --> 00:44:04,440
it will freeze, it will break, and it will fail.

557
00:44:05,820 --> 00:44:09,300
You see, bridges are not only part of the natural landscape,

558
00:44:09,480 --> 00:44:11,000
I would like also to underline that.

559
00:44:11,400 --> 00:44:14,140
They are also very much part of a cultural landscape,

560
00:44:14,340 --> 00:44:16,220
so they are also part of our culture,

561
00:44:16,760 --> 00:44:21,520
and the new generations will judge us as well,

562
00:44:21,521 --> 00:44:24,920
or as bad according to the quality of the bridges that we have there.

563
00:44:26,480 --> 00:44:32,920
And perhaps, according to the sincerity of our desire to reach across and join.

564
00:44:35,940 --> 00:44:39,480
Our president was gone. Generals were in rebellion.

565
00:44:39,920 --> 00:44:42,780
In 30 days, we would cease to exist as a nation.

566
00:44:43,140 --> 00:44:47,240
But a handshake and one man's word changed history forever.

567
00:44:47,900 --> 00:44:50,060
It wasn't a month. It was a miracle.

568
00:44:50,061 --> 00:44:51,900
April 1865

569
00:44:51,901 --> 00:44:53,660
April 14th at 9

570
00:44:53,661 --> 00:44:55,220
Only on the History Channel

